## Proof

The statement is **true**.

**Case 1: $f = 0$ (the zero functional).** Then $H = \{x \in X : 0 = r\} = \emptyset$ since $r > 0$. Hence there is no $z \in H$, and the statement holds vacuously.

**Case 2: $f \neq 0$.** Since $f$ is a nonzero linear functional into $\mathbb{R}$, it is surjective, so every level set $H_n = \{x : f(x) = r_n\}$ is nonempty. Let $\|f\| = \sup_{\|x\|\le 1} |f(x)|$ denote the operator norm of $f$; since $f \neq 0$, we have $\|f\| \in (0, \infty]$ (in fact finite, since $f$ is continuous).

Fix $z \in H$, so $f(z) = r$. For any $x \in H_n$ we have $f(x) = r_n$, hence $f(z - x) = r - r_n$. Setting $y = z - x$, we compute

$$d(z, H_n) = \inf_{x \in H_n} \|z - x\| = \inf\{\|y\| : f(y) = r - r_n\}.$$

**Lower bound.** For any $y$ with $f(y) = r - r_n$, the definition of the operator norm gives $|f(y)| \le \|f\|\,\|y\|$, so

$$\|y\| \ge \frac{|f(y)|}{\|f\|} = \frac{|r - r_n|}{\|f\|}.$$

Taking the infimum, $d(z, H_n) \ge \frac{|r - r_n|}{\|f\|}$.

**Upper bound.** If $r = r_n$, then $0 \in \{y : f(y) = 0\}$, so $d(z, H_n) = 0 = \frac{|r-r_n|}{\|f\|}$.

If $r \neq r_n$: by definition of the operator norm, for every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $u \in X$ with $\|u\| = 1$ and $|f(u)| > \|f\| - \varepsilon$. Replacing $u$ by $-u$ if necessary, we may assume $f(u)$ has the same sign as $r - r_n$. Set

$$y_\varepsilon = \frac{r - r_n}{f(u)}\, u.$$

Then $f(y_\varepsilon) = r - r_n$ and

$$\|y_\varepsilon\| = \frac{|r - r_n|}{|f(u)|} < \frac{|r - r_n|}{\|f\| - \varepsilon}.$$

Letting $\varepsilon \to 0$ (with $\varepsilon < \|f\|$), we obtain $d(z, H_n) \le \frac{|r - r_n|}{\|f\|}$.

**Combining**, $d(z, H_n) = \frac{|r - r_n|}{\|f\|}$. Since $r_n \to r$, we have $|r - r_n| \to 0$, and therefore

$$d(z, H_n) = \frac{|r - r_n|}{\|f\|} \longrightarrow 0 \quad \text{as } n \to \infty.$$

This holds for every $z \in H$, completing the proof. $\blacksquare$

